<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  happy-dsh — hand-written reference LaunchAgent.

  This is the version to edit yourself. `install.sh` generates the same shape
  with your real paths filled in; use that one unless you want to own the file.

  ── Install ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    cp ai.happy-dsh.web.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
    launchctl bootstrap gui/$UID ~/Library/LaunchAgents/ai.happy-dsh.web.plist
    launchctl print          gui/$UID/ai.happy-dsh.web      # inspect
    launchctl bootout        gui/$UID/ai.happy-dsh.web      # stop and unload

  ── What you must change ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    1. Every `/Users/YOU` below.
    2. `/usr/local/bin/dsh` → wherever your dsh lives. Check with
       `command -v dsh`. If you run from a checkout instead of a global
       install, use the node form:
           <string>/opt/homebrew/bin/node</string>
           <string>/Users/YOU/happy-dsh/apps/cli/lib/bin.js</string>
    3. `dsh.example.com` → the authority your browser will use. It must match
       what you put in your proxy and your local DNS. Add more <string> values
       after it if you have several.
    4. PATH — see the note on that key below.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>ai.happy-dsh.web</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/usr/local/bin/dsh</string>
    <string>web</string>

    <!--
      ── ORDER MATTERS — read this before reordering anything ────────────────
      `--patch` is a LAUNCHER flag (apps/cli/src/args.ts), not a `dsh web`
      flag. The launcher parses its own flags first and stops at the first
      token it does not recognise, handing everything after that to the app:

          "The launcher's flags come first and end at the first token it does
           not know; everything from there on belongs to the booted app."

      So every launcher flag must come BEFORE the first app flag. Put
      `--no-open` above `--patch` and the launcher has already stopped
      listening — the service starts and dies instantly with

          error: unknown option '--patch'

      `--trusted-host` must stay LAST: it is declared variadic
      (`<authority...>`) and swallows every following token that does not
      begin with a dash.
    -->

    <!--
      Recommended when the browser is NOT on this machine: the host's own
      desktop affordances are wrong for a remote browser. The directory picker
      would open an OS dialog on this Mac's screen that you cannot see or
      answer, and "Open in…" would spawn a file manager here.

          deploy/remote-access/overlays/remote-browser.yml

      Delete this pair if you only ever use the UI from this Mac's own browser.
    -->
    <string>--patch</string>
    <string>/Users/YOU/happy-dsh/deploy/remote-access/overlays/remote-browser.yml</string>

    <!-- Nobody is at the console; do not try to open a browser. -->
    <string>--no-open</string>

    <!-- Optional. Pin the port, or drop both lines to take the default (3080). -->
    <string>--port</string>
    <string>3080</string>

    <string>--trusted-host</string>
    <string>dsh.example.com</string>
  </array>

  <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
  <string>/Users/YOU</string>

  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <!--
      PATH is baked in at install time and is load-bearing: agent turns spawn
      real shell commands, so a service running with launchd's minimal default
      PATH (roughly /usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin) will fail to find
      homebrew's git, node, rg, and so on. Copy the output of
      `echo $PATH` from your login shell, and re-run the installer after you
      add a new tool that lives in a new place.

      This is the same sharp edge ollama's installer has — it bakes
      `Environment="PATH=$PATH"` from whatever shell ran the install. It is a
      real tradeoff, not an oversight: the alternative is a curated PATH list
      that goes stale in a different, quieter way.
    -->
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>

    <key>DSH_HOME</key>
    <string>/Users/YOU/.dsh</string>

    <!--
      Service identity, so a process can tell that it is running inside the
      supervised service. restart.sh reads these to decide whether an in-place
      restart needs a detached handoff.

      The marker alone is NOT proof of ancestry: a detached handoff inherits it
      while running outside the job. restart.sh passes an explicit --detached
      flag to its helper rather than re-deriving membership, because
      re-deriving it would make the helper detach itself again, forever.
    -->
    <key>HAPPY_DSH_SERVICE</key>
    <string>1</string>
    <key>HAPPY_DSH_LAUNCHD_LABEL</key>
    <string>ai.happy-dsh.web</string>
  </dict>

  <!--
    RunAtLoad starts the service when you log in.

    A LaunchAgent is a per-user, per-session job: it does not run before your
    login, and it is torn down when the session ends. Starting a process as
    you *before you log in* needs a LaunchDaemon, which runs as root — the
    wrong shape for an agent that must act as you, with your credentials and
    your file access. Log in once and it stays up from there.
  -->
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    KeepAlive true, not {SuccessfulExit: false}: ANY exit comes back.

    DSH exits 0 on SIGTERM, but a clean exit is also the simplest idiom for
    "restart me" — a plugin that needs a restart can just let the process exit
    and let launchd bring it back. Making every exit recoverable is what makes
    that work, and it is the whole reason the unit does not need a restart
    helper for the common case.

    The cost: killing the process no longer leaves the service down. To
    actually stop it, unload the job:

        launchctl bootout gui/$UID/ai.happy-dsh.web

    ...or use restart.sh --park, which also disables it for the next login.
    This mirrors systemd's Restart=always, which likewise ignores an explicit
    "systemctl stop" only because systemd special-cases it.
  -->
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    launchd's restart rate limit, in seconds: it will not respawn this job more
    often than this.

    ── launchd throttles. It does NOT give up. ──────────────────────────────
    Measured, not assumed: a job that exits 1 immediately, with this
    ThrottleInterval and the KeepAlive below, was still being respawned after
    60 seconds — `runs` climbed 2 → 3 → 5 → 6 and `state` stayed
    `spawn scheduled`. launchd never stopped trying.

    systemd is different: StartLimitIntervalSec + StartLimitBurst make it give
    up and mark the unit `failed`. There is no launchd equivalent setting.

    So on macOS a permanently broken configuration is throttled to roughly six
    attempts a minute, forever. That is not a flood, but it is unbounded: the
    appended stderr grows without limit and the machine keeps paying for the
    restarts.

    The real fix is the exit-code contract DSH does not implement yet. When it
    lands, the macOS mechanism is a wrapper that maps "config is broken" (78)
    to exit 0, so the SuccessfulExit=false below reads it as a clean stop and
    stops respawning. That is the technique OpenClaw documents. Until then,
    this interval is only a rate limit — the service will keep retrying a
    configuration that cannot work.
  -->
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
  <integer>10</integer>

  <!--
    How long launchd waits after SIGTERM before SIGKILL.

    DSH's own guarantee is the input: apps/cli/src/process-shutdown.ts declares
    PROCESS_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_MS = 5000, after which it forces exit — and a
    second signal escalates immediately. 15s is that contract plus margin, and
    deliberately the same number as the systemd unit's TimeoutStopSec so the
    two platforms behave alike. (launchd's own default is 20s.)
  -->
  <key>ExitTimeOut</key>
  <integer>15</integer>

  <!--
    Applied when launchd CREATES StandardOutPath / StandardErrorPath below.
    63 decimal is 0o077, so those files are born 0600 rather than 0644.

    This is what makes the log containment real. Without it, the very first
    launch on a machine where the files do not exist yet creates them
    world-readable, and the launch token is exposed for the life of the file —
    no amount of pre-creating them survives a user who deletes and restarts.
    Verified: deleting both files and restarting produces 0600, not 0644.
  -->
  <key>Umask</key>
  <integer>63</integer>

  <!--
    A server holding a WebSocket open should not be treated as background work,
    or launchd may throttle its timers.
  -->
  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Interactive</string>

  <!--
    Nothing here needs a terminal. An inherited tty would make interactive
    prompts and /dev/tty probes misbehave under a supervisor.
  -->
  <key>StandardInPath</key>
  <string>/dev/null</string>

  <!--
    ── The point of this whole file ──────────────────────────────────────────
    On boot DSH prints its URL with a launch token:

        dsh web: http://…?token=<opaque>

    That token mints a session cookie for ANY trusted authority. It is not
    single-use and it does not expire — it is valid until the process exits.
    Treat the line as a password.

    A log file the supervisor creates for you is written with the process
    umask, so by default a first launch leaves ~/.dsh/serve.stdout at 0644 —
    readable by every account on the Mac, and by anything that indexes your
    home directory. The Umask key above is what prevents that: the file is
    born 0600.

    install.sh also pre-creates both files at 0600 before loading the job.
    That is now belt-and-braces rather than load-bearing, and it is worth
    keeping: it means the directory is 0700 and the files exist and are
    properly owned before launchd ever touches them.

    If you are installing this file by hand, do the same — it costs four lines
    and removes a whole class of surprise:

        mkdir -p ~/.dsh && chmod 700 ~/.dsh
        : > ~/.dsh/serve.stdout && chmod 600 ~/.dsh/serve.stdout
        : > ~/.dsh/serve.stderr && chmod 600 ~/.dsh/serve.stderr

        mkdir -p ~/.dsh && chmod 700 ~/.dsh
        : > ~/.dsh/serve.stdout && chmod 600 ~/.dsh/serve.stdout
        : > ~/.dsh/serve.stderr && chmod 600 ~/.dsh/serve.stderr

    Then read your URL back with:

        grep -Eo 'https?://[^ ]*token=[^ ]*' ~/.dsh/serve.stdout | tail -1

    Note the file only grows on restart (one URL line per boot), so it stays
    small. Truncate it any time with `: > ~/.dsh/serve.stdout`.
  -->
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/Users/YOU/.dsh/serve.stdout</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/Users/YOU/.dsh/serve.stderr</string>
</dict>
</plist>
